{"conjecture": "Specifications whose computational content is extracted from Gödel–Dialectica-style realizability witnesses yield programs whose correctness is mechanically guaranteed by the proof, yet current extraction is blind to resource usage and fails to exploit the partial-evaluation opportunities present in the witness's computational trace. Embedding a cost-aware partial-evaluation pass directly into the realizability translation—refining the witness before extraction by specializing recursive calls against statically known argument fragments—will produce synthesized code that is not only provably correct but runs in measurably fewer reduction steps than the unrefined extracted program. The conjecture is that for the class of specifications whose proofs induce strictly positive recursive witnesses, this cost-aware refinement preserves realizability while achieving at least a constant-factor reduction in asymptotic operational cost for a nontrivial subclass including tree-recursive traversals over statically shaped inputs."}
